chine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.\n\nIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n\n\n## B03.1\n\n**Check:** Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Workload token exchange is runtime and short-lived, but the Anthropic API key source is not visible (no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY env or secret in deployment.yaml), and Slack/mail secrets are static mounts.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B03.2\n\n**Check:** Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Workload token expires in 600s (deployment.yaml:107); STS expires_in and rotation of Slack and mail HMAC secrets are set outside this repo.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B03.3\n\n**Check:** No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** Dockerfile:12 — \"ENV PYTHONPATH=/opt/site:/app \\\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Dockerfile and manifests carry no secret literals\n\n`Dockerfile` lines 1–22 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n   1  # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.10\n   2  FROM python:3.11.10-slim-bookworm@sha256:2a6386ad2db20e7f55073f69a98d6da2cf9f168e05e7487d2670c22e4ad2a1bd AS build\n   3  WORKDIR /src\n   4  ENV PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1\n   5  COPY pyproject.toml ./\n   6  COPY app ./app\n   7  RUN pip install --target /opt/site .\n   8  RUN python -m compileall -q app\n   9  \n  10  FROM gcr.io/distroless/python3-debian12:nonroot@sha256:8f3c1a6e2b9d4f7a0c5e8b1d3f6a9c2e5b8d1f4a7c0e3b6d9f2a5c8e1b4d7f0a\n  11  WORKDIR /app\n  12> ENV PYTHONPATH=/opt/site:/app \\\n  13      PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \\\n  14      APP_ROOT=/app\n  15  COPY --from=build /opt/site /opt/site\n  16  COPY --from=build /src/app /app/app\n  17  COPY prompts /app/prompts\n  18  COPY config /app/config\n  19  COPY release/manifest.yaml /app/release/manifest.yaml\n  20  USER 65532:65532\n  21  EXPOSE 8080\n  22  ENTRYPOINT [\"python\", \"-m\", \"uvicorn\", \"app.main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8080\", \"--no-server-header\"]\n```\n\n## B03.4\n\n**Check:** Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** STS-issued tokens are sent as plain Bearer (mcp_client.py:77); whether STS or gateways support DPoP or mTLS binding is not stated in this repo.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to B03.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B03\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B03.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n```\nVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\n","numLines":101,"startLine":1,"totalLines":101}}}
